          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Ungual fold—crescent-shaped tissue surrounding the proximal claw.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Coronary band and dorsal ridge (dorsal ridge of the ungual crest)—produces most of the claw; contributes to the curvature of the claw.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Paronychia—inflammation of soft tissue around the claw.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Onychomycosis—fungal infection of the",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Onychorrhexis—brittle claws that tend to split or break.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Onychomadesis—sloughing of the claw.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Onychodystrophy—deformity caused by abnormal growth; often a sequela of a disorder.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Onychomalacia—softening of the claw.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Claws and ungual folds—subject to trauma, infection, vascular insufficiency, immunemediated disease, parasites, neoplasia, defects in keratinization, and congenital abnormalities.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A particular claw deformity may be caused by a variety of diseases—claw disease is most often associated with other dermatoses: rarely the only epithelial structure affected.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "A single disease can present with various claw lesions.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "One foot affected—top differentials include foreign body, neoplasia, trauma, fungal infection, bacterial infection.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Multiple feet affected—allergy, endocrine, secondary infections, demodicosis, immune-mediated disorders, epidermolysis bullosa, symmetric lupoid onychodystrophy (SLO), plasma cell pododermatitis, superficial necrolytic dermatitis, nutritional deficiencies, psychogenic dermatoses, idiopathic.",
              "pdf_page": 364,
              "printed_page": 282,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
